In chainer/cupy, creating your own custom kernel is as easy as:
@cp.util.memoize(for_each_device=True)
def _get_fill_c_next_and_h_kernel():
    cuda_lib = cp.cuda.compile_with_cache("""

        extern "C" __global__ void fill_c_next_and_h(
                       const float* a, const float* i, const float* f, const float* o,
                       const float* c_prev, float* c_next, float* h, 
                       int num_batches,
                       int num_samples,
                       int hidden_size) 
        {
            int batch_idx  = threadIdx.x + blockIdx.x * blockDim.x;
            int sample_idx = threadIdx.y + blockIdx.y * blockDim.y;
            int hidden_idx = threadIdx.z + blockIdx.z * blockDim.z;

            if (batch_idx < num_batches && sample_idx < num_samples && hidden_idx < hidden_size){

                int idx = (batch_idx*num_samples*hidden_size
                           + sample_idx*hidden_size
                           + hidden_idx);

                c_next[idx] = i[idx]*a[idx]+c_prev[idx]*f[idx];
                h[idx]      = o[idx]*tanhf(c_next[idx]);
            }
        }
       """)
    return cuda_lib.get_function('fill_c_next_and_h')

And then calling it with:
def get_memory_cell_and_output_batched(a, i, f, o, c_prev):

    num_batches = a.shape[0]
    num_samples = a.shape[1]
    hidden_size = a.shape[2]
    c_next = cp.empty_like(c_prev)
    h = cp.empty_like(c_prev)

    fill_c_next_and_h = _get_fill_c_next_and_h_kernel()

    bdim = (4,16,8)
    gdim = (int(math.ceil(num_batches/4.0)), 
            int(math.ceil(num_samples/16.0)),
            int(math.ceil(hidden_size/8.0))
            )
    fill_c_next_and_h(grid=gdim, block=bdim,
                      args = (
                              a, i, f, o,
                              c_prev, c_next, h,
                              num_batches,
                              num_samples,
                              hidden_size
                              )
                      )

    return c_next, h

It could be cool if something similar was possible in pytorch.

I tried writing a custom op and defining a module to wrap it. However, I came across a tedious problem which I feel useful to document here and provide some suggestions.
I started with a custom op that does very simple reduction as follows
import torch

class MyFunction(torch.nn.Function):
	def forward(self, input):
		self.shape = input.size()
		return input.mean(dim=1)
	def backward(self, grad_out):
		return grad_out.expand(self.shape) / float(self.shape[1])

class MyModule(torch.nn.Module):
	def __init__(self):
		self.op = MyFunction()

	def forward(self, input):
		return self.op(input)         
There you may have noticed that I have made a mistake by saving the instance in the module, provided you have read the documentation carefully and found this line

Each function is meant to be used only once (in the forward pass).

at http://pytorch.org/docs/autograd.html#function
However, the above snippet ran without any problem in my model under the single GPU mode, i.e. when no torch.nn.DataParallel is used.
When I started adding DataParallel to my model, the nightmare began. Pytorch repeatedly threw error like this
RuntimeError: arguments are located on different GPUs at ...

Once I remove the DataParallel module, the model can run smoothly.
Since this error happens in the C++ side, there is no stack trace to help debug this problem. I got no clue which arguments lead to the error and had to doubt whether it is a bug in the DataParallel module.
After spending a lot of time digging into the sources of DataParallel and even THC , I finally realized the mistake I made. So I changed the code to
import torch

class MyFunction(torch.nn.Function):
	def forward(self, input):
		self.shape = input.size()
		return input.mean(dim=1)
	def backward(self, grad_out):
		return grad_out.expand(self.shape) / float(self.shape[1])

class MyModule(torch.nn.Module):

	def forward(self, input):
		return MyFunction()(input)
Then everything began to work as expected.
I have a few comments afterward:

If it is bound to fail in the multi-GPU situation, maybe we should also let it fail in the single GPU case.
The mentioned line in the Doc should state this clearly that if we reuse a Function instance there might be some serious problem.
One step further, if an instance of Function is meant to be used only once, how about integrating it into the extension mechanism? This may potentially protect many new users from facing this kind of problems.

After all, thanks to the team for developing this awesome toolbox!
P.S. I apologize if you feel uncomfortable about the tab indent in the code snippet.
